-- Approval controls - who is allowed to sign off on what, and how a
-- system can prove it three quarters from now.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain. Every business of any size needs people
-- to approve things, and not everyone may approve everything. Some
-- approvals are simple sign-offs: onboarding a new vendor, agreeing a
-- policy change. The only question is whether the person doing the
-- approving holds authority for that kind of document - a compliance
-- officer may, a marketing intern may not. Other approvals are about
-- money: invoices, purchase orders, expense claims. Now the question
-- grows a clause - does the person have authority *up to the amount*?
-- A junior clerk might approve invoices up to a small ceiling, a
-- finance manager a larger one, the CFO larger still. A real
-- organisation runs both shapes side by side, and an auditor will one
-- day ask: who approved this payment, under what authority, and was
-- that authority still current on the day?
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about it. The system never stores bare
-- "facts" - it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they
-- obey the rules. Authority itself is one of those claims: "this
-- person may approve invoices" is something the system has admitted,
-- and it can later be retracted. Two ideas do the real work below:
--
--   * The *actor* is simply whoever is proposing a change - the person
--     whose hand is on the action right now. The approve actions below
--     do not take the approver as a parameter you pass in; the runtime
--     already knows who is acting, and the action consults that.
--   * A `require` is a gate on a single action, checked at the moment
--     you act and never again. Authority belongs in a gate, not in a
--     standing rule. That distinction is the whole point of this
--     example: a document approved today must stay approved even if
--     the approver loses their authority tomorrow. If "approvals must
--     be backed by live authority" were a standing rule over all
--     state, then revoking someone's authority would suddenly break
--     every document they ever approved - or force the system to erase
--     that history. Neither is honest. So there are deliberately *no*
--     standing rules about authority here; every approval is checked
--     once, at the moment it is made, and the record then stands on its
--     own.
--     Each gate below is written with a name before its condition,
--     and a refused action is reported under that name.
--   * The approver chooses WHICH document to approve, never the facts
--     that decide whether they may. A document is put on the record
--     first, once, with its kind and - if it is about money - its
--     amount. The approval reads both from the record, so nobody can
--     approve an invoice as if it were a vendor onboarding, or state a
--     smaller amount than the invoice carries.

program approval_controls

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold. The first two are about who *may* approve (the authority,
-- which can be granted and revoked); the last two are the durable
-- record of an approval that actually happened (append-only - they are
-- never retracted). Note the `actor` slot: the recorded approval
-- stamps in who did the approving.
predicate MayApprove(actor: Subject, doc_type: Subject)
-- Approval records are append only - they are never retracted, and
-- now the model SAYS so: revoking authority stops future approvals
-- (the gate consults live authority), while every approval already
-- granted stays in the record, machine-guaranteed.
predicate Approval(doc_id: Subject, doc_type: Subject, actor: Subject)
    append only
predicate ApprovalLimit(actor: Subject, doc_type: Subject, limit: Decimal)
predicate LimitedApproval(doc_id: Subject, doc_type: Subject, amount: Decimal, actor: Subject)
    append only

-- The documents themselves. A document's kind, and its amount if it has
-- one, are fixed the moment it is put on the record: one kind and one
-- amount per document, never changed afterwards. A document with an
-- amount can only be approved against a ceiling. If an invoice's amount
-- really changes, that is a new document, not an edit to this one.
predicate Document(doc_id: Subject, doc_type: Subject)
    unique by (doc_id)
    append only
predicate DocumentAmount(doc_id: Subject, amount: Decimal)
    unique by (doc_id)
    append only

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits - "authority was granted", "a document was approved".
-- They are how this corner of the business tells the rest of it, and
-- any downstream systems, what just happened.
intent ApprovalAuthorityGranted(principal: Subject, doc_type: Subject)
intent ApprovalAuthorityRevoked(principal: Subject, doc_type: Subject)
intent DocumentApproved(doc_id: Subject, actor: Subject)
intent ApprovalLimitGranted(principal: Subject, doc_type: Subject, limit: Decimal)
intent ApprovalLimitRevoked(principal: Subject, doc_type: Subject, limit: Decimal)
intent DocumentApprovedWithinLimit(doc_id: Subject, actor: Subject, amount: Decimal)


-- ===========================================================
-- Putting a document on the record.
-- ===========================================================

-- Each registers a document once. The gate refuses an id already on the
-- record, so a document cannot gain an amount later and slip from one
-- approval route to the other. Amounts here are positive: credit notes
-- and refunds would need rules of their own. Whoever submits is trusted
-- to record the document's true kind and amount - checking that against
-- the paper invoice is a separate control.
transformation submit_document(doc_id, doc_type):
    require document_id_unused: not Document(doc_id, _)
    admit Document(doc_id, doc_type)

transformation submit_monetary_document(doc_id, doc_type, amount):
    require document_id_unused: not Document(doc_id, _)
    require amount_is_positive: 0 < amount
    admit Document(doc_id, doc_type)
    admit DocumentAmount(doc_id, amount)


-- ===========================================================
-- Unconditional authority - the simple sign-off, no amount involved.
-- ===========================================================

-- Grant someone the authority to approve a kind of document. This adds
-- the authority claim.
transformation grant_approval_authority(principal, doc_type):
    admit MayApprove(principal, doc_type)
    emit ApprovalAuthorityGranted(principal, doc_type)


-- Take that authority away. The gate insists the authority is actually
-- held before it can be removed, then retracts it. Revoking only stops
-- *future* approvals; documents already approved stay approved.
transformation revoke_approval_authority(principal, doc_type):
    require authority_is_held: MayApprove(principal, doc_type)
    retract MayApprove(principal, doc_type)
    emit ApprovalAuthorityRevoked(principal, doc_type)


-- Approve a document. Its kind comes from the record, and a document
-- with an amount is refused here: it must go through its ceiling. The
-- gate checks that whoever is proposing this - the actor - holds the
-- matching authority right now. If they do, the approval is recorded
-- with their identity stamped on it; if not, the whole action is
-- refused and nothing is recorded.
transformation approve_document(doc_id):
    bind Document(doc_id, doc_type)
    require document_has_no_amount: not DocumentAmount(doc_id, _)
    require actor_may_approve_the_kind: MayApprove(actor, doc_type)
    admit Approval(doc_id, doc_type, actor)
    emit DocumentApproved(doc_id, actor)


-- ===========================================================
-- Quantitative authority - approval up to a money ceiling.
-- ===========================================================

-- Grant someone an approval ceiling for a kind of document - "may
-- approve invoices up to this amount". The same person can be given
-- ceilings for different document kinds.
transformation grant_approval_limit(principal, doc_type, limit):
    admit ApprovalLimit(principal, doc_type, limit)
    emit ApprovalLimitGranted(principal, doc_type, limit)


-- Remove a specific approval ceiling. The gate requires that exact
-- grant to exist before it can be taken away.
transformation revoke_approval_limit(principal, doc_type, limit):
    require ceiling_is_held: ApprovalLimit(principal, doc_type, limit)
    retract ApprovalLimit(principal, doc_type, limit)
    emit ApprovalLimitRevoked(principal, doc_type, limit)


-- Approve a document against a ceiling. Its kind and amount come from
-- the record, not from the approver. The gate does two things at once:
-- it finds an approval ceiling the proposing actor holds for this
-- document kind, and it checks the amount fits within that ceiling
-- (the comparison is inclusive - exactly at the ceiling is allowed).
-- Pass the gate and the approval is recorded with the actor and the
-- authorised amount; fail it and nothing is recorded.
transformation approve_within_limit(doc_id):
    bind Document(doc_id, doc_type)
    bind DocumentAmount(doc_id, amount)
    require amount_within_actors_ceiling: ApprovalLimit(actor, doc_type, limit) and amount <= limit
    admit LimitedApproval(doc_id, doc_type, amount, actor)
    emit DocumentApprovedWithinLimit(doc_id, actor, amount)
